--TEST--
Core\Arr::contains has no loose mode and its strict mode is one numeric domain, so it matches neither of in_array's two, and keyOf's ?string removes array_search's === false trap
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
Three divergences from one decision, and none of them is about which entry is found. First, `in_array`/`array_search` take a third argument that selects loose or strict comparison and default to *loose*; spec § 2 names `contains` "(always strict)" and there is no third argument here to forget, so MWL has no spelling for PHP's default at all. Second, MWL's strict is not PHP's strict: ADR 0090 § 3 makes `int`, `uint`, `float` and `decimal` one numeric domain, so `contains([1.0], 1)` is `true` where `in_array(1, [1.0], true)` is `false` — the same reading that keeps this member and the `==` operator one comparison rather than two, and `mwl_core_arr_contains`'s doc comment owns it. Third, `keyOf` answers `?string` rather than `string|int|false` (ADR 0063 R5), so the key `"0"` and "not found" are two different values instead of two values PHP's own manual has to warn are `==`-equal; the last block below is the `array_search(...) == false` bug, written out in a language that cannot express it.
--FILE--
<?mwl
final class Show {
    public static function key(?string $k): string {
        if ($k == null) {
            return "none";
        }
        return $k as string;
    }

    public static function yes(bool $b): string {
        if ($b) {
            return "yes";
        }
        return "no";
    }
}

// The numeric domain. `in_array(1, [1.0, 2.5], true)` is `false`, because PHP's
// strict mode compares the *type* first; `in_array(1, [1.0, 2.5])` is `true`,
// because its loose mode converts. MWL answers `yes` and it is neither of those
// modes — the value `1` and the value `1.0` are one value here, which is the
// same rule `1 == 1.0` already follows in an expression.
array<int|float> $numeric = [1.0, 2.5];
echo Show::yes(Core\Arr::contains($numeric, 1)), "|", Show::key(Core\Arr::keyOf($numeric, 1)), "\n";

// The half of that rule PHP's loose mode does *not* share: a string is never a
// number here. `in_array("1", [1, "1"])` is `0` under the loose reading — it
// finds the *integer* — where MWL finds the string it was given.
array<int|string> $mixed = [1, "1"];
echo Show::key(Core\Arr::keyOf($mixed, "1")), "|", Show::key(Core\Arr::keyOf($mixed, 1)), "\n";

// `in_array("abc", [0, 1])` was `true` before PHP 8 and is `false` after, so the
// loose mode's answer here is a property of the runtime's version rather than of
// the data. MWL has one answer and it does not move.
array<int|string> $zeroish = [0, 1];
echo Show::yes(Core\Arr::contains($zeroish, "abc")), "|", Show::yes(Core\Arr::contains($zeroish, "0")), "\n";

// `null` is a value that can be looked for, not an answer, and it does not
// collapse into `false`, `0` or `""` the way `in_array`'s loose mode would.
array<?string> $holes = ["a", null, ""];
echo Show::key(Core\Arr::keyOf($holes, null)), "|", Show::key(Core\Arr::keyOf($holes, "")), "\n";

// The `=== false` trap, written out. In PHP `array_search(10, [10, 20]) == false`
// is `true`, because the key `0` and the sentinel `false` are `==`-equal — which
// is why its manual tells every caller to use `===`. Here the answer is a
// `?string`, so the only thing `== null` can mean is "not found", and the first
// entry's key does not answer it. The two lines below are the found and the
// absent case put through exactly the same test.
array<int> $pair = [10, 20];
echo Show::yes(Core\Arr::keyOf($pair, 10) == null), "|", Show::key(Core\Arr::keyOf($pair, 10)), "\n";
echo Show::yes(Core\Arr::keyOf($pair, 99) == null), "|", Show::key(Core\Arr::keyOf($pair, 99)), "\n";
--EXPECT--
yes|0
1|0
no|no
1|2
no|0
yes|none
